-## Physical sigma and defocus identifiability
+## Effective process-screen MTF and physical focus
 
-Uniform periodic screen areas constrain the **combined** scanner transfer very
-well, but they do not in general contain enough independent spatial-frequency
-information to split that transfer uniquely into both residual Gaussian
-`sigma` and physical image-plane `defocus`.  On a real Paget scan, materially
-different sigma/defocus pairs produced essentially the same residual because
-the strong surviving screen harmonics sample only a small part of the MTF.
-The result then depends on the optimizer start even when every selected area is
-fitted simultaneously.
-
-For a physical diffraction model, multi-area focus analysis therefore refuses
-to optimize scanner-MTF `sigma` and `defocus` at the same time.  Calibrate or
-fix one component and optimize the other.  In particular, a slanted-edge MTF
-measurement can provide the residual sigma before the screen areas are used to
-fit defocus.  Conversely, keep defocus fixed when the purpose of the area fit
-is to estimate a residual sigma.
-
-This restriction is deliberate: choosing an arbitrary continuation or
-multistart winner would make the numerical residual slightly smaller while
-still reporting a physically non-identifiable decomposition.
+The sharpness quantity that matters most to additive-screen colour recovery is
+the capture transfer at the process-screen frequency.  Sigma and defocus are
+therefore a representation of the fitted transfer rather than the primary
+diagnostic.  Different physical parameter pairs are acceptable when they
+produce essentially the same useful modulation at the screen carrier.
+
+Candidate verification fits the local screen phase independently in each area.
+The shared focus fit then keeps those verified phases fixed.  This separation is
+important: moving all local phases in the same simplex as scanner MTF lets
+registration absorb part of the periodic modulation and can drive a cold focus
+fit toward an unrealistically sharp transfer.
+
+For monochrome/BW analysis with both scanner-MTF `sigma` and defocus requested,
+the shared fit uses small scalar blocks instead of one coupled simplex.  It
+first refines sigma, which is a monotone coordinate for the useful carrier MTF,
+with the caller's defocus fixed.  A few cheap scalar restarts avoid premature
+termination on a shallow shoulder.  Defocus is then allowed to improve the
+transfer shape with sigma fixed, followed by another sigma polish only when the
+defocus step genuinely improves the pixel residual.  The final result reports
+`process_screen_frequency` and `process_screen_mtf` explicitly.
+
+This works for both intended starting conditions.  With no slanted-edge
+calibration, the area fit estimates the useful transfer from a cold physical
+model.  With an existing MTF calibration, the same procedure starts from that
+model and can estimate additional degradation introduced by the actual
+historical-object capture, for example cover glass or a small focus mismatch.
+The exact split between extra sigma and extra defocus remains secondary to the
+resulting carrier MTF.
+
+For coupled BW fits, leave-one-out validation freezes the joint defocus/compact
+blur coordinate and refits the monotone residual sigma on each retained subset.
+The resulting process-screen MTF span and maximum displacement measure whether
+the colour-recovery transfer itself is stable without repeatedly choosing a new
+physical decomposition.
 
 ## Qt workflow
 
